adds a test to verify that there is more than one entry in CC_FIELDS.

Hugo
--- ReadPassword.pm.old	2005-10-25 19:56:02.000000000 +0100
+++ ReadPassword.pm	2007-11-04 15:03:54.000000000 +0000
@@ -4,8 +4,8 @@
 use Term::ReadLine;
 use POSIX qw(:termios_h);
     use constant CC_FIELDS =>
-	(VEOF VEOL VERASE VINTR VKILL VQUIT
-	VSUSP VSTART VSTOP VMIN VTIME NCCS);
+	(VEOF, VEOL, VERASE, VINTR, VKILL, VQUIT,
+	VSUSP, VSTART, VSTOP, VMIN, VTIME);

 use vars qw(
     $VERSION @ISA @EXPORT @EXPORT_OK
@@ -19,7 +19,7 @@
 @EXPORT = qw(
 	read_password 
 );
-$VERSION = '0.07';
+$VERSION = '0.08';

 # The special characters in the input stream
 %SPECIAL = (
--- Changes.old	2005-08-28 21:08:59.000000000 +0100
+++ Changes	2007-11-04 15:03:38.000000000 +0000
@@ -21,3 +21,6 @@
 0.06  Sun Aug 28 13:07:00 2005
 	- Setting $Term::ReadPassword::USE_STARS to a true value now
 	  echoes stars for password characters.
+
+0.07  Sun Nov 04 15:46:00 2007
+	- CC_FIELDS list was broken (Hugo van der Sanden )
--- META.yml.old	2005-10-25 19:56:10.000000000 +0100
+++ META.yml	2007-11-04 15:03:48.000000000 +0000
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 # http://module-build.sourceforge.net/META-spec.html
 #XXXXXXX This is a prototype!!!  It will change in the future!!! XXXXX#
 name:         Term-ReadPassword
-version:      0.07
+version:      0.08
 version_from: ReadPassword.pm
 installdirs:  site
 requires:
--- t/1_basics.t.old	2005-03-31 21:27:40.000000000 +0100
+++ t/1_basics.t	2007-11-04 15:04:10.000000000 +0000
@@ -1,7 +1,15 @@
 #!perl

-BEGIN { $| = 1; print "1..1\\n"; }
+BEGIN { $| = 1; print "1..2\\n"; }
 END {print "not ok 1\\n" unless $loaded;}
 use Term::ReadPassword;
 $loaded = 1;
 print "ok 1\\n";
+{
+    my @list = Term::ReadPassword::CC_FIELDS;
+    if (@list > 1) {
+	print "ok 2\\n";
+    } else {
+	print "not ok 2\\n"
+    }
+}

input_text:
Subject: Re: [perl #47107] English.pm namespace pollution bug

Dave Mitchell wrote:
>> blead has the same problem.  Patch is attached.  The solution was to simply
>> only export the scalar values, not the whole glob.  @ARG gave trouble, it's
>> value wasn't being retained when exported as @ARG rather than *ARG.  I suspect
>> @_ magic.  So ARG() will continue to be clobbered.
> 
> It aliases the whole glob for a reason: so that local(), and aliasing
> mechanisms such as for, preserve the mapping between short and long variable
> names.
> 
> if you alias just the glob's scalar slot, it goes wrong:

I didn't know that.  But I didn't do it that way.  The full glob is aliased,
but only the scalar is exported.

However, I see that breaks localization, too... sometimes.  It's weird.
$WARNING and $^W still work but things like $FORMAT_NAME and $LIST_SEPARATOR
don't.

Anyhow, the patch should be reversed.  In its place, put this new test for the
localization.

--- lib/English.t       (/vendor/bleadperl)     (revision 40850)
+++ lib/English.t       (/local/bleadperl)      (revision 40850)
@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@
     @INC = '../lib';
 }

-use Test::More tests => 55;
+use Test::More tests => 57;

 use English qw( -no_match_vars ) ;
 use Config;
@@ -158,6 +158,16 @@
 main::ok( !$MATCH, '$MATCH disabled' );
 main::ok( !$POSTMATCH, '$POSTMATCH disabled' );

+
+# Check that both variables change when localized.
+{
+    local $LIST_SEPARATOR = "wibble";
+    ::is $", 'wibble', '$" changes when $LIST_SEPARATOR is localized';
+
+    local $" = 'frooble';
+    ::is $LIST_SEPARATOR, 'frooble';
+}
+
 __END__
 This is a line.
 This is a paragraph.


-- 
Reality is that which, when you stop believing in it, doesn't go away.
	-- Phillip K. Dick

